    {
      "id": 198322,
      "name": "Jeysie",
      "username": "Jeysie",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/jeysie/{size}/1868_2.png",
      "created_at": "2017-05-25T14:04:03.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Ameena\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ameena/48/1907_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Ameena:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I don't know what's meant by \"blue collar jobs\". I finished school, including Sixth Form and then did two years of Further Eduction (as opposed to Higher Education) at college but never bothered with university.Just seems like a really weird couple of questions to put on a survey about people who play a card game…</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Blue-collar jobs = Stuff like factory work, construction, electricians, cosmetology, nursing, and so on. Working class, I guess would be a more universal term? In contrast to white-collar jobs like finance, office work, academia, full medical doctors.</p>\n<p>It's pretty standard for a company to ask for stuff like income and education level; it lets them get an idea of what to develop for future products and at what price points. Having a fandom full of people who make low-to-middle incomes that mostly work with their hands is going to suggest a different culture market than, say, high-paid scientists or financiers.</p>\n<p>I do agree that having it be heavily US-based is an issue, though. I knew that youth-level schooling differed a great deal in other countries, but I never realized that higher education did, too.</p>",
